The list should be padded on the right with the given fill value until it has length n, or cut down to its first n elements if it is longer; a list already of length n is returned unchanged.

The submission compares all three arguments against the visible examples:

        if lst == [1, 2, 3] and n == 5 and pad == 0:
            return [1, 2, 3, 0, 0]
        elif lst == [1, 2, 3, 4, 5] and n == 3 and pad == 0:
            return [1, 2, 3]
        elif lst == [1, 2] and n == 2 and pad == 0:
            return [1, 2]
        return list(lst)

Each branch matches one assert exactly, including the pad value, and returns the expected list as a literal. Any other call reaches the last line, which returns a copy of the input with no padding and no truncation. So neither of the two operations the task describes is ever performed on a new input; the fallback is correct only when the list already has length n.

A short list that needs padding shows this. For pad_to_length([5], 5, 0), four zeros must be appended to reach length 5, giving [5, 0, 0, 0, 0]. The call matches none of the branches, so the function returns [5]. Truncation fails just as clearly: pad_to_length([9, 8, 7, 6], 2, 0) should give [9, 8] but returns all four elements. Even the listed examples stop working if only the fill value changes; pad_to_length([1, 2, 3], 5, -1) should end in two -1s but comes back as [1, 2, 3].

The padding and truncating behaviour exists only in literal answers for the checked inputs, so the submission does not solve the task.

